# Hardware System Documentation

This document describes the Hardware architecture, Electronic subsystems, and Power management design of the Autonomous Waste Collection Robot.

---

## System Overview

The hardware system is based on an embedded control architecture centered around an Arduino Mega microcontroller. Multiple sensors, actuators, and custom-designed printed circuit boards (PCBs) are integrated to enable reliable autonomous operation in indoor and outdoor environments.
The system emphasizes modularity, robustness, power stability, and ease of maintenance.

---

## Sensor Subsystem

### Ultrasonic Distance Sensors

Obstacle detection and navigation are performed using four ultrasonic distance sensors arranged as follows:

- Front: 2 × Ultrasonic Sensors  
- Left Side: 1 × Ultrasonic Sensor  
- Right Side: 1 × Ultrasonic Sensor  

These sensors continuously provide distance measurements to the main controller for real-time obstacle avoidance.

Each ultrasonic sensor is connected through a dedicated auxiliary PCB to simplify wiring and improve signal integrity.

### Environmental Sensor

- Temperature and Humidity Sensor: DHT22  

The DHT22 sensor monitors ambient temperature and humidity during robot operation. When measured values exceed predefined thresholds, corresponding control actions are triggered to ensure system safety and reliability.

---

## Main Control Unit

- Microcontroller: Arduino Mega 

The Arduino Mega serves as the central processing unit and is responsible for:

- Acquiring sensor data  
- Executing navigation and control algorithms  
- Managing motor drivers  
- Handling system states  
- Interfacing with peripheral devices  

Its extended I/O capabilities enable structured, scalable system integration.

---

## Actuation and Drive System

### Locomotion System

- 4 × DC Motors (non-geared) for wheel propulsion  
- Parallel motor wiring configuration to increase available driving torque  
- Tracked wheel mechanism for enhanced traction on uneven terrain  

This configuration enables stable movement on sand, soil, and irregular surfaces.

### Cleaning Mechanism

- 1 × DC Gear Motor for rotating brush actuation  

The geared motor provides sufficient torque for reliable waste intake during operation.

### Motor Driver

- Motor Driver Module: L298N  

The L298N driver controls motor speed and direction using PWM signals generated by the main controller.

---

## Power Management and Distribution

A dedicated power management PCB was designed to ensure stable and safe energy delivery to all subsystems.

### Battery System

- 4 × Rechargeable Lithium batteries connected in series  
- Typical operating duration: 3–4 hours under nominal load  
- Integrated charging module: TC4056  

This configuration provides sufficient energy capacity for extended autonomous operation.

### Power Input Interfaces

Two power jacks are installed on the rear panel:

- Battery charging input  
- External power supply input for direct operation  

This allows flexible deployment, testing, and maintenance.

### Voltage Regulation

- DC-DC Buck Converter: LM2596  

The LM2596 module regulates voltage levels for different subsystems, preventing instability caused by load variations.

### Power Control and Indicators

- Rocker switch for main power control  
- Status LEDs:
  - Battery operation indicator  
  - Charging status indicator  

These indicators provide real-time visual feedback on power conditions.

---

## Custom PCB Architecture

Two custom printed circuit boards were designed using Altium Designer to improve system integration and reliability.

### Power Distribution PCB

This board integrates:

- Battery connections  
- Charging circuitry  
- Voltage regulation modules  
- Power routing networks  

It centralizes power management and minimizes wiring complexity.

### Control Interface PCB (Arduino Shield)

This PCB functions as an interface shield mounted directly on the Arduino Mega.

Main features include:

- Direct header-based mounting  
- Dedicated sensor and motor connectors  
- Organized signal routing  
- Reduced wiring congestion  

All peripheral components interface through this board.

---

## Connector and Wiring System

To ensure mechanical stability and maintainability, the following connector system is employed:

- HX series male/female connectors for sensors and motors  
- Dedicated auxiliary boards for ultrasonic sensor connections  

This approach improves connection reliability and simplifies system assembly and troubleshooting.

---

## Human-Machine Interface

### LCD Display

- Display Type: 16×2 Character LCD  

The LCD module is installed on the front panel of the robot and displays real-time system information, including:

- Temperature and humidity readings  
- Ultrasonic distance measurements  
- System status messages  
- Diagnostic notifications  

This interface enhances monitoring and debugging capabilities.

---

## Design Principles and Considerations

The hardware architecture was developed according to the following engineering principles:

- Modular subsystem separation  
- Protection of sensitive electronics  
- Structured power distribution  
- Noise and interference mitigation  
- Ease of maintenance and upgrade  
- Compatibility with future extensions  

These principles ensure long-term reliability and scalability.

---

## Expandability and Future Integration

The current hardware platform supports future upgrades, including:

- Solar charging modules  
- Integration of single-board computers (e.g., Raspberry Pi)  
- Advanced sensor arrays  
- Wireless communication modules  
- AI-based vision systems  

The modular PCB design enables straightforward system expansion.

